<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- The entitlements the shipped local bundle signs with.

     P1-012 started from an empty set and let the experiment decide. With no
     entitlement at all, a hardened-runtime ad-hoc bundle cannot even load the Qt
     libraries this project built: every nested library is ad-hoc signed with no
     team identifier, the hardened runtime leaves library validation on, and dyld
     refuses the load ("mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have
     different Team IDs"), so the shell aborts before it starts. Disabling library
     validation is therefore not a convenience here: without it neither the
     shipped bundle nor the LGPL substitution route works under the hardened
     runtime.

     The cost is explicit: this bundle accepts any code signature on a library it
     loads, which is what protects a recipient's rebuilt LGPL Qt library, and it
     buys that openness by giving up dyld's check that every loaded library carries
     the same signing identity as the main executable. For a local-only open-source
     build the second property was never available (there is no team identity to
     validate against); a Developer ID or notarized distribution would revisit it,
     and those are out of scope for this project. See
     packaging/macos/strict-library-validation.entitlements.plist for the variant
     the experiment signs one copy with, and docs/tasks/p1-012-*.md for the
     recorded results. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
